  "Source": "R. P. Netterfield, \"Practical thin-film polarizing beam-splitters,\" Optica Acta 24(1), 69-79 (1977). doi:10.1080/713819379 - Fig. 2 caption; Section 2.1 text p. 72",
  "Notes": "Cemented-cube polarizing beam-splitter, 633 nm; 9 layers, equal optical thickness at 45 deg\nSpecification: Polarizing efficiency > 99.8 % in reflection and transmission at 633 nm; Rp = 0 at 633 nm\nReported: Rp = 0 at 633 nm and at 452.5 nm, Rp peaks (~19 %) at 528 nm (Fig. 2); Ts = 0.06 % at 633 nm; Ts zero near 863.5 nm and 380 nm.\nNotes: Symmetric stack; incident and exit media are both the cube glass/cement, n = 1.57. The 45 deg angle is in the n = 1.57 glass (diagonal face). Thicknesses are physical; checked: n d cos(theta) = 132 nm = 528/4 for both H and L at 45 deg in n=1.57. Indices are given at 633 nm only (non-dispersive constants assumed); the layers are non-absorbing.\nLayers from Fig. 2 caption; Section 2.1 text p. 72; layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nChecked with ThinFilmDesigner (2026-10-21), agrees with the paper: ours Ts 0.063 %, Rp 5.0e-5 % at 633 nm; paper Ts 0.06 %, Rp ~0 (theory).",
